{- | `pds/serve.mdk` — the PDS entry point: admit a configuration, rehydrate the
   account repository from disk, bind the listener, and serve.

   Every configuration value arrives as a command-line flag and is admitted
   before anything is bound: a bad DID, handle, or hostname is rejected by
   `lib.server_core`'s `makeAccount`, an unreadable or non-hex signing key by
   `readHexBytes`, a data directory that is not a directory by `requireDir`,
   and a repository whose head does not verify under the configured key by
   `lib.repo`'s `repoFromBlocks`. Every one of those exits nonzero. There is no
   default for any of them, so a misconfigured server never starts serving
   something other than what was asked for.

   The bind address is `--bind`, defaulting to `127.0.0.1` (loopback). A
   non-loopback address is refused before anything is read or written unless
   `--trusted-proxy` is also given (`requireTrustedBind`): this process has no
   way to observe whether the peer on the other end of an accepted connection
   is actually the reverse proxy `--trusted-proxy` says it is — there is no
   `getpeername` in the runtime — so the flag is an operator assertion about
   the deployment, not a verified fact, and the refusal is what makes an
   unproxied direct bind require deliberately opting out of it rather than
   silently exposing the server (`#2757`,
   `docs/design/ATPROTO-PDS-DESIGN.md`).

   One data directory is served by one process. `--data` is locked
   (`shell.dirlock`) before anything beneath it is read or written, so a second
   server started over a live directory is refused rather than sweeping the
   staged files the first one has in flight. A killed process leaves its lock
   behind — `mkdir(2)` releases nothing on death — so the lock is graded by the
   holder's heartbeat and not by its existence, and an ordinary restart
   reclaims an abandoned one on its own. `--force-lock` skips that wait.

   The signing key is read here and handed to `lib.repo`, which is the only
   thing that needs it, and to nothing else. It is never printed, and neither is
   the `Store` that transitively holds it.

   The session-token secret is the SECOND secret this entry point holds: the
   server's own 32-byte HS256 key, which `lib.server_core`'s seam verifies
   bearer tokens against. It is deliberately a different key from `--key`: the
   signing key authenticates commits to every other implementation on the
   network and has exactly one job. `--token-secret PATH` supplies it; with no
   flag, the secret is generated from `osEntropyBytes` on first run and kept in
   `<data>/session-secret` for every run after.

   The account password is the THIRD, and it is what `com.atproto.server.
   createSession` grades a login against. It arrives as `--password-file PATH`
   and NEVER as an argument value: an argument is visible in `ps` output to
   every user on the box. There is no interactive prompt either — no termios,
   tty, or echo-suppression primitive exists in the runtime or the stdlib, and
   a prompt that echoed the password to the terminal would be worse than the
   file, not better. The password is read once, at bootstrap, turned into a
   PBKDF2 credential record (`lib.credential`), and never stored: `<data>/
   credential` holds a salt, an iteration count, and a derived key. A run whose
   data directory already holds that record needs no `--password-file` at all.

   Every file this program creates holding a secret — the generated session
   secret and the credential record — is written at mode 0600, and a secret
   file it READS at any wider mode is refused before the listener binds rather
   than used: a signing key or session-token secret that another account on
   the box can read is not a secret, and serving anyway would be
   indistinguishable from a correctly permissioned deployment right up until
   somebody used it.

   A failed configuration leaves nothing behind either: every secret the flags
   SUPPLY is read and admitted before any secret this program GENERATES is
   written, so a run rejected for a bad password file leaves no session secret
   on disk for the next run to adopt. That invariant covers the durable EVENT
   LOG as well as the secrets: recovering a half-finished append is decided
   with the rest of the admissions and performed only after the last step that
   can still refuse, so a refused run mutates the log no more than it mutates
   the secrets. -}

import args.{
  Args,
  ArgSpec,
  flag,
  flagValue,
  flagValues,
  intValue,
  parseArgs,
  spec,
  switch,
  value,
}
import array as A
import async.{Async, liftIO, sleep, spawn}
import fs.{isDir}
import hex.{decode, encode}
import io.{isPrivateMode, writeFilePrivate}
import lib.blob.{validMimeShape}
import lib.credential.{
  CredentialRecord,
  credentialFromParts,
  defaultIterations,
  makeCredential,
